WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.660
Grains slide.

00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:03.843
Peas roll.

00:00:04.714 --> 00:00:06.064
Beans interlock.

00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:11.561
Robot-assisted feeding helps people
with motor impairments eat.

00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:16.456
But for a robot, every spoonful
of granular food is a physics problem.

00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:26.509
It starts in simulation.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:30.827
Seven granular foods, with varied shapes
and physical properties,

00:00:30.877 --> 00:00:36.890
are modeled using GPU-accelerated physics
in NVIDIA Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab.

00:00:38.544 --> 00:00:43.611
Next, an operator demonstrates scooping
and transport directly in virtual reality,

00:00:43.661 --> 00:00:47.318
and, for comparison,
with a 3D space mouse.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:53.093
Each interface yields 120 demonstrations,
about 40 minutes of data.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:58.725
From these demonstrations, we train
three imitation-learning methods:

00:00:58.775 --> 00:01:02.880
Behavioral Cloning, its recurrent variant,
BC-RNN,

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.143
and Batch-Constrained Q-learning, or BCQ.

00:01:09.726 --> 00:01:12.143
Two questions guide the evaluation.

00:01:12.193 --> 00:01:14.485
Which learning method performs best?

00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:17.141
And does the demonstration
interface matter?

00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:22.100
Policies are tested on foods
with very different dynamics,

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.695
and benchmarked against a human expert.

00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:30.046
Peas, beans, and flakes
were never seen during training.

00:01:35.245 --> 00:01:37.844
Policies trained on VR demonstrations

00:01:37.894 --> 00:01:41.427
consistently outperformed those
trained with the space mouse.

00:01:41.477 --> 00:01:47.139
BCQ performed best overall,
reaching 80% success with VR data,

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:52.801
versus 68%, and it generalized
most strongly to unseen foods.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.505
Trained on VR data, BCQ also spilled
about half as much:

00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:01.330
8 pieces on average, versus 15.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.363
And with vectorized environments, many
robots can train and evaluate in parallel.

00:02:13.002 --> 00:02:15.656
Better demonstrations, better policies.

00:02:15.706 --> 00:02:18.766
Find the paper and code
on the project page.
